 = Conclusion
 
+This report has explored how learning to reverse a gradual noise corruption process enables generative image synthesis, tracing the foundational progression from pixel-space DDPM @ho2020ddpm to high-resolution LDM @rombach2022ldm. In answering the guiding question of this seminar, diffusion models establish that generative synthesis can be framed as a tractable, stable regression task over noise vectors rather than an unstable adversarial minimax optimization or an autoregressive factorization. By training a neural network to predict the injected Gaussian noise across intermediate diffusion timesteps via a simplified mean squared error objective, the model effectively learns to estimate the score function of smoothed data distributions. During ancestral sampling, iteratively subtracting these predicted noise increments along the reverse Markov chain steers pure Gaussian entropy back toward high-density regions of the data distribution, synthesizing sharp and diverse samples.
+
+The transition from DDPM to LDM represents an essential architectural evolution designed to overcome the prohibitive computational burden of diffusing directly across pixel space. By decoupling the generative pipeline into perceptual compression via a spatial autoencoder and semantic generation within the compressed latent space, LDM eliminates high-frequency spatial redundancies while preserving core semantic structures. Our empirical benchmark on $28 times 28$ images demonstrated that compressing images by a factor of sixteen in area yields a $14.19times$ reduction in floating-point operations per step and a $6.13times$ reduction in peak memory, while multi-head cross-attention provides a flexible mechanism to steer an identical latent noise realization toward semantically divergent classes. Crucially, our empirical profiling also highlighted a key practical nuance: latent compression primarily reduces arithmetic demands rather than end-to-end sampling latency at low resolutions, where sequential kernel launch overhead and autoencoder decoding dominate the critical path.
+
+Several promising directions remain open for further exploration. First, inference latency remains the primary obstacle for real-time applications; non-Markovian deterministic samplers such as Denoising Diffusion Implicit Models (DDIM) @song2020ddim, progressive distillation, and flow matching offer compelling solutions to compress the thousand-step sampling trajectory into a handful of function evaluations. Second, generative backbones are increasingly transitioning from convolutional U-Nets to Diffusion Transformers (DiT) @peebles2023dit, which exhibit superior compute scaling behavior, global receptive fields, and parameter efficiency across higher-dimensional latent manifolds. Finally, deploying high-resolution generative models responsibly necessitates continued advancements in dataset curation, bias mitigation, and provenance attribution frameworks to address copyright vulnerabilities and societal asymmetries inherent in web-scale multimodal training.
