 === Data, Copyright, and Bias
 
-Our experiments are trained exclusively on MNIST, released under a Creative Commons Attribution-ShareAlike licence; no web-scraped corpus, photograph or third-party artwork enters the pipeline, so the copyright exposure of the present study is minimal. Its limitations are nonetheless real: the corpus is demographically narrow, produced by a predominantly adult American population whose handwriting conventions differ markedly from other cultures, and audits report a small fraction of incorrect labels. A model trained on it inherits a narrow prior over stroke shape and orientation, visible in our samples as clean but stylistically uniform digits, so any claim of generalisable generation quality must be restricted to the MNIST domain.
+Our experiments are trained exclusively on the MNIST benchmark dataset @lecun1998mnist, released under a Creative Commons Attribution-ShareAlike 3.0 (CC BY-SA 3.0) licence; no web-scraped corpus, photograph, or third-party artwork enters the pipeline, so the copyright exposure of the present study is minimal. Its limitations are nonetheless real: the corpus is demographically narrow, originating from NIST Special Database 3 and 1 collected from US Census Bureau employees and high school students @lecun1998mnist, whose handwriting conventions differ markedly from other cultures. Furthermore, systematic benchmark audits report a small fraction of label errors (approximately 0.15% in test sets) due to ambiguous or incorrect manual annotations @northcutt2021pervasive. A model trained on it inherits a narrow prior over stroke shape and orientation, visible in our samples as clean but stylistically uniform digits, so any claim of generalisable generation quality must be restricted to the MNIST domain.
 
-The literature motivating this report is exposed to risks our setting avoids. The high-resolution latent diffusion models of @rombach2022ldm and their successors are trained on web-scraped image-text collections of billions of pairs in which copyrighted artworks and identifiable persons appear unfiltered, which raises a copyright problem, since near-verbatim training images can be extracted from a trained generator; a style problem, since the manner of an identifiable artist can be reproduced without copying any image exactly, leaving attribution and consent unresolved; and a bias problem, since these corpora over-represent the English-speaking and affluent parts of the internet, an asymmetry that cross-attention can even steer deliberately. Countermeasures include deduplication and opt-out lists, provenance metadata such as the Coalition for Content Provenance and Authenticity (C2PA) standard, and auditing outputs for memorised content.
+The literature motivating this report is exposed to risks our setting avoids. The high-resolution latent diffusion models of @rombach2022ldm and their successors are trained on web-scraped image-text collections of billions of pairs, such as LAION-5B @schuhmann2022laion, in which copyrighted artworks and identifiable persons appear unfiltered. This raises a copyright problem, since near-verbatim training images can be extracted from a trained generator @carlini2023extracting; a style problem, since the manner of an identifiable artist can be reproduced without copying any image exactly, leaving attribution and consent unresolved; and a bias problem, since these corpora over-represent the English-speaking and affluent parts of the internet, an asymmetry that cross-attention can even steer deliberately. Countermeasures include deduplication and opt-out lists, provenance metadata such as the Coalition for Content Provenance and Authenticity (C2PA) standard @c2pa2023spec, and auditing outputs for memorised content.
